+---
+name: release-notes
+description: Write Quillnote's weekly customer-facing release notes from pull requests merged since the last notes, saved to release-notes/YYYY-MM-DD.md. Use for the Friday release notes, "what shipped this week" summaries for customers, or redoing/fixing a week's notes.
+---
+
+# Quillnote weekly release notes
+
+Turn the week's merged pull requests into short, plain-language notes for Quillnote customers. Writes one file in the product repo; never commits, pushes, or publishes it.
+
+Not for: internal changelogs, engineering status updates, or versioned library release notes. Those need different audiences and rules.
+
+## 1. Collect the merged PRs
+
+From the product repo root, run:
+
+```sh
+python3 scripts/collect_prs.py --repo <product-repo>
+```
+
+(`scripts/` is relative to this skill's folder.) It prints JSON with `since`, `source`, and each PR's `number`, `title`, `labels`, `files`, and `body`.
+
+- **Range.** By default it starts from the date of the newest earlier `release-notes/*.md` file, or the previous Friday if there are none. Use `--since YYYY-MM-DD` when the user names a different range, for example after a skipped week. Tell the user which range you used.
+- **`source: "gh"`** gives you real labels. **`source: "git"`** means the GitHub CLI was unavailable, so `labels` is `null`. In that case infer the category from title prefixes (`chore:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `ci:`, `build:`, `docs:`) and changed files, and say in your report that labels were not checked.
+- If the script can't run, use `git log --merges --first-parent --since=<date>` plus `git diff --name-only <merge>^1 <merge>` to get the same facts.
+- If there are no PRs in range, skip to the empty-week case in step 4.
+
+## 2. Filter out internal work
+
+Drop any PR labeled `internal`, `chore`, `refactor`, or `test`. Also drop it when there are no labels and the title prefix or the files show it's one of those (for example, it only touches tests, CI, or build config). Drop pure docs, dependency bumps, and other tooling work too, unless the body describes a change customers will notice.
+
+Judge by what changed, not by how exciting the title sounds. A refactor titled "New sync engine" is still a refactor. This exact mistake has happened before. When a PR's label and its content disagree, trust the label for exclusion and mention the conflict in your report.
+
+## 3. Classify and rewrite each remaining PR
+
+Choose exactly one group per item:
+
+- **New** means customers can do something they could not do before, or something now behaves differently on purpose.
+- **Improved** means something that already existed is now faster, clearer, or easier to use.
+- **Fixed** means something that was broken now works the way it was always supposed to.
+
+"Fixed" is only correct when the old behavior was a bug. If the PR adds new behavior or changes intended behavior, it belongs in New or Improved even when its title says `fix:`. Read the body and diff summary when you're unsure.
+
+Rewrite each title as a customer-facing outcome. Say what the user notices, not what the code does.
+`fix: null check in importer` → `Imports from Apple Notes no longer fail on empty notes.`
+
+**Platform tag.** For every item, decide which platforms it affects. If only iOS, start the item with `[iOS]`. If only web, start it with `[Web]`. Use no tag when it affects both. Base this on changed paths (for example `ios/`, `*.swift`, `web/`, `*.tsx`), labels, and the body. Missing tags are a known mistake, so check every item. If you can't tell, leave the item untagged and list it in your report as an unverified platform.
+
+Several PRs that make one customer-visible change should become one line.
+
+## 4. Write the file
+
+Save the notes to `release-notes/<today, YYYY-MM-DD>.md` in the product repo. If that file already exists, show the user how yours differs and ask before overwriting it.
+
+```markdown
+# Quillnote release notes: <Month D, YYYY>
+
+## New
+- [iOS] ...
+
+## Improved
+- ...
+
+## Fixed
+- [Web] ...
+```
+
+Leave out any group that has no items. Keep the whole file under 200 words, counting the heading. If it runs over, merge related items and cut detail. Don't drop customer-facing changes silently. If you have to drop some, report which ones.
+
+**Empty week:** if nothing customer-facing is left after filtering, the file contains only this line:
+
+```
+Small fixes and performance work this week.
+```
+
+## 5. Verify before reporting
+
+Check the saved file against this list and fix anything that fails:
+
+- [ ] Every item traces back to a PR that survived the filter, and no internal, chore, refactor, or test PR made it in.
+- [ ] No customer names, engineer names or GitHub handles, internal ticket IDs (such as `ABC-123` or `#123`), PR numbers, or branch names appear. Merge-commit text like "from alice/branch" is a common way these leak.
+- [ ] Every item labeled Fixed corrects a bug, not new behavior.
+- [ ] Every item that affects only iOS or only web has its tag.
+- [ ] The file has fewer than 200 words (`wc -w`).
+- [ ] The path is `release-notes/YYYY-MM-DD.md` with today's date.
+
+## Report to the user
+
+Reply briefly with:
+
+- the file path and the notes themselves;
+- the date range, and whether labels came from GitHub or were inferred;
+- the excluded PRs, as number and short reason, so the user can catch a wrong exclusion;
+- any items with uncertain grouping or platform, plus any label conflicts.
+
+Leave committing and publishing to the user.
